Graph - Adjacency List

This project demonstrates how to represent an undirected graph using an adjacency list in C++.

Problem

Store the connections between vertices efficiently and display all neighboring vertices for each node.

Approach

* Created a `Graph` class.
* Used an array of linked lists (`list<int>`) to store the neighbors of each vertex.
* Added edges in both directions to represent an undirected graph.
* Printed the adjacency list by traversing each linked list.

Time Complexity

* Graph Creation: O(V)
* Add Edge: O(1)
* Print Adjacency List: O(V + E)

Space Complexity

O(V + E)

What I Learned

* What a graph is and where it is used.
* Difference between vertices and edges.
* Why adjacency lists are preferred for sparse graphs.
* How to create an undirected graph using linked lists.
* How `list<int>*` works as an array of linked lists.
* How range-based for loops traverse the neighbors of a vertex.

